WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.450
A body is found in the neighbourhood one morning; no one knows who did it.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.700
The Ottoman court accuses no one, but it charges the whole neighbourhood for the death.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:17.990
So — are you obliged to pay the bill for an injustice committed by a grandfather you never met, or do you have a right to your share of his glory?

4
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:32.080
In the first grey light of a winter morning, a man is lying face down in the middle of the street.

5
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.530
There is no snow, only frost; the blood on the stones has frozen.

6
00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:47.540
The neighbourhood watches from behind its doors, and nobody wants to take the first step, because everybody knows the same thing: if the killer of this body is never found, the neighbourhood will pay for him.

7
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.550
This scene is not from a novel.

8
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:55.320
It comes from a legal practice that ran for centuries in the Ottoman city.

9
00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:06.330
Islamic law has an institution called kasâme, a procedure of collective oath-taking: when a body is found somewhere and the killer is unknown, the people of that place cannot stand outside the matter.

10
00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:15.100
In Hanafi practice, 50 men from the quarter or the village swear an oath in the name of God: we did not kill him, and we do not know who did.

11
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.470
The oath does not clear them.

12
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:24.800
After the oath, the adult men of that place pay the dead man's blood money, the diyet, dividing it among themselves.

13
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.810
A debt spread over three years.

14
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.140
There is no culprit, but there is a bill.

15
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:38.790
The registers of imperial rulings kept in the Ottoman archives show that this was not an abstract question for law books.

16
00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:49.160
In the correspondence sent to the capital from the districts of Rumelia in the 18th century, you can read line by line how these cases were conducted, which village paid for which body.

17
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:56.330
Which means that a baker who had stood over his dough all night long could still end up paying for a corpse found in his street at dawn.

18
00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:00.140
Paying for a death that nobody had ever accused him of causing.

19
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.150
The first reaction is anger.

20
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.680
What kind of justice is this?

21
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.970
But consider, for a moment, the logic behind the institution.

22
00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:14.260
The neighbourhood was the smallest security unit of its age.

23
00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:21.270
It was the thing that closed its gates at night, hired its own watchman, questioned the stranger, broke up the brawl.

24
00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:26.520
A murder with no known killer is proof that the neighbourhood did not do its own job.

25
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:33.050
The blood money is not a punishment but an invoice: you did not keep your eyes open, and this is the price.

26
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:39.900
The institution does not aim to punish a criminal; it aims to hold a community answerable for its own street.

27
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.950
And it works.

28
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:47.040
A neighbourhood that knows it will be the one paying moves heaven and earth to find the killer.

29
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.850
We like to think we live entirely outside this logic today.

30
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.900
We do not.

31
00:02:52.900 --> 00:03:02.630
If a piece breaks off the façade of an apartment building and lands on a passer-by, nobody asks who poured that concrete; the owners collectively shoulder the damage.

32
00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:08.840
The mistake of a single employee is paid out of the company's accounts, not out of that person's pocket.

33
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:15.690
When the state loses a compensation case, the money comes from taxpayers, not from the official who made the decision.

34
00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.500
Modern law did not abolish collective responsibility.

35
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.430
It rewrote it in a politer, colder, more institutional language.

36
00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:30.000
But the example of the neighbourhood has a limit: a neighbourhood is a partnership in space.

37
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.410
You stand on the same street, you live through the same night, you hire the same watchman.

38
00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.420
Responsibility spreads sideways.

39
00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:43.510
The question of this episode, however, is not horizontal but vertical.

40
00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:46.360
Not in space, but in time.

41
00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:56.090
Because the genuinely uncomfortable question is this: what if the partnership is not built on the same street but in the same blood, the same surname, the same flag?

42
00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:03.740
What if the body was not found in this morning's street but in the street of 80 years ago, and you had not even been born yet?

43
00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.990
If your grandfather did something to someone, does any residue of it pass to you?

44
00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:14.880
As a shame, a debt, an obligation to apologise, or even just as a discomfort?

45
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:21.250
Most people answer this question immediately, and they do it by flinging themselves to one of two extremes.

46
00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:31.140
One side says: no one can be held answerable for what their father did, this is a self-evident principle of conscience, and the opposite view is the mentality of the blood feud.

47
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:38.790
The other side says: a people cannot move forward without reckoning with its own past, and the grandchild who keeps silent is an accomplice.

48
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.960
Each position looks coherent from the inside.

49
00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.930
And notice that both of them use the same word: guilt.

50
00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:49.580
The whole matter lies in that word.

51
00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:59.790
In the middle of the 20th century, after his own country had carried out one of the greatest destructions in history, a philosopher bent over precisely this word and noticed something:

52
00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:04.240
with a single term we are describing at least four separate things.

53
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:11.410
And unless those four are pulled apart from one another, every sentence spoken about the grandfather's crime hangs in the air.

54
00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.060
The winter of 1945.

55
00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.870
Heidelberg University is reopening among the ruins.

56
00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:28.440
A professor of philosophy who had been removed from his chair during the war because of his Jewish wife, and whose name had appeared on deportation lists, Karl Jaspers,

57
00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:34.170
walks into a lecture hall and announces the subject of his course: the guilt of the Germans.

58
00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:39.100
Sitting in front of him are young men who a year earlier had worn the uniform of that regime.

59
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:46.270
The following year these lectures become a book, Die Schuldfrage, in English The Question of German Guilt.

60
00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:53.280
It is a thin volume, and one of the texts in the history of philosophy whose weight is most inversely proportional to its page count.

61
00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.570
What Jaspers does is not an accounting but a separation.

62
00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:04.580
Out of the blurred feeling we call guilt he draws four distinct layers, and he appoints a different judge to each one.

63
00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.950
The first is criminal guilt.

64
00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:12.520
You have committed an act: you gave the order, you pulled the trigger, you locked the door.

65
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.770
Its judge is the court, its measure is the law, its outcome is punishment.

66
00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.740
This layer is entirely individual and cannot be transferred.

67
00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:26.030
Your grandfather's file is your grandfather's file.

68
00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.720
The second is political guilt.

69
00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.650
To be the citizen of a state is to take on the consequences of what that state does.

70
00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:43.860
You may not have voted for it, you may have opposed it, you may even have gone to prison for opposing it; the burden of what was done in the name of the state still falls to the citizens of that state.

71
00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:51.510
Its judge is not conscience but power, politics, treaties; its outcome is not punishment but reparation and a reconstituted order.

72
00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:55.960
Here Jaspers uses the word burden, not the word sin.

73
00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.970
The third is moral guilt.

74
00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:02.300
The reckoning in the place the law never reaches.

75
00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:09.470
Closing the curtain while your neighbour is taken away, carrying on with something you knew to be wrong because it was easier, keeping quiet.

76
00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:16.960
No one else can be the judge of this; the only court is the person's own conscience and those friends honest enough to help them look at it.

77
00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:23.490
No verdict of moral guilt can be issued about an entire people, because conscience does not come in the plural.

78
00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:27.460
The fourth, and the strangest, is metaphysical guilt.

79
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:39.270
Human beings stand in solidarity with one another; if an injustice happened somewhere and you went on living, then even if there was nothing you could have done, a wound opens inside you that does not close.

80
00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:43.560
Its judge, according to Jaspers, is God alone.

81
00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:51.050
The philosopher deliberately steps outside the law here, because what he is describing is not a penalty but a shaking of the ground.

82
00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:57.260
The real value of these four layers is this: in argument, people constantly change layers.

83
00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:08.910
Someone who wants to refuse the political burden escapes into the criminal layer by saying "I never killed anyone." Someone who wants to convict a whole people leaps from the political burden to moral guilt.

84
00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:17.200
There are four separate scales, and people keep lifting the weight off one and setting it down on another, and the result comes out wrong every time.

85
00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:25.010
Hannah Arendt, Jaspers's student and his correspondent for the rest of his life, took this distinction and sharpened it further.

86
00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:32.660
In an essay written as early as 1945 she argued that the idea of collective guilt is far more dangerous than it first appears.

87
00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:35.670
Because if everyone is guilty, no one is.

88
00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:44.440
The sentence "all Germans are guilty" sounds like the heaviest possible accusation; in practice it is the broadest possible acquittal.

89
00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:56.250
The moment you place the man who gave the order and the man who closed his curtain, the engineer of the camp and the villager who knew nothing, on the same side of the scale, you lose the ability to judge any of them.

90
00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:00.380
Guilt, once universalised, evaporates.

91
00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:04.350
This is why Arendt draws a hard line between two words.

92
00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:12.640
Guilt is individual: it attaches to an act, to an agent, to an intention; it cannot be shared and cannot be inherited.

93
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:22.210
Responsibility, on the other hand, can be collective: it is the price of belonging to a community, and it finds you even if you committed no act.

94
00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:33.060
Arendt grounds this as follows: we do not live our lives alone but among others; we receive from a community its language, its trust, its protection, its inheritance.

95
00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:37.350
Whoever takes that inheritance takes the other page of the ledger as well.

96
00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:44.680
This was one of the reasons that Eichmann in Jerusalem, the book Arendt wrote after watching the trial there, attracted so much anger.

97
00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:54.090
When she described the man sitting across from her not as a monster but as a functionary who never thought about the meaning of what he was doing, she was assumed to be shrinking the crime.

98
00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:59.820
In fact she was doing exactly the opposite: she was handing the crime back to the person.

99
00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:08.910
Not to the spirit of an age, not to the current of history, not to the character of a people, but to that man, at that desk, with that signature.

100
00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:12.880
Now place this distinction on the grandfather's table.

101
00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:18.130
If your grandfather bore criminal guilt, it is not yours and cannot be.

102
00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:22.420
If he bore moral guilt, its judge died long ago.

103
00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:29.270
But the political burden, meaning the consequences of that act that are still standing today, may still be sitting somewhere.

104
00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:32.920
That is the page on which the grandchild appears.

105
00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:35.770
Not as a culprit, but as an heir.

106
00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:41.980
Western philosophy arrived at this distinction in the 20th century by walking through a terrible destruction.

107
00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:47.710
But the principle itself, in its clearest and sternest form, sits in a far older sentence.

108
00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:54.720
The Qur'an repeats it in different chapters in almost the same words: no bearer of burdens bears the burden of another.

109
00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:02.210
The word used in this phrase, which appears in the chapters of al-An'am, al-Isra and Fatir, tells us a great deal.

110
00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:07.940
Wizr means a heavy pack carried on the back; wazira is the one who shoulders that pack.

111
00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:12.070
Which is to say that the scene has been a porter's scene from the beginning.

112
00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:15.080
Everyone carries his own load.

113
00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:21.610
The chapter of al-Najm takes it one step further: a person has nothing but what he himself has worked for.

114
00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:29.100
And the chapter of al-Muddathir compresses the whole accounting into a single image: every soul is held in pledge for what it has earned.

115
00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:32.910
A pledge is what you leave behind as security for a debt.

116
00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:36.400
No one is left in pledge for another man's debt.

117
00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:44.050
This principle is a direct intervention into one of the most powerful institutions of pre-Islamic Arab society: the blood feud.

118
00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:55.380
In tribal logic there is no difference between the killer and the killer's lineage; when someone is killed, vengeance may be taken from the killer's relatives too, and indeed ought to be.

119
00:11:55.380 --> 00:12:00.150
What the verse says cuts that chain: the reckoning belongs to the person.

120
00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:03.640
And here is where the tension lies.

121
00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:08.090
Within the same legal tradition there is an institution called the âkile.

122
00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:19.420
The âkile is a person's circle of close kin and mutual support; when someone causes a death by accident, without intent, this circle divides the blood money among themselves and pays it.

123
00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:26.590
Which means that the cost of an accident caused by one man's hand is paid by 20 relatives who had nothing whatsoever to do with it.

124
00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:34.400
A tradition that says no bearer of burdens bears the burden of another is, on the same page, organising payment by others.

125
00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.770
Is this a contradiction?

126
00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:44.900
Islamic jurisprudence answers with a very clean distinction, and that distinction overlaps strikingly with Jaspers's four layers.

127
00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:55.110
Uqubah, punishment, is personal: retribution, imprisonment, sin, the reckoning in the hereafter cannot be transferred, divided or inherited.

128
00:12:55.110 --> 00:13:01.800
Daman, the compensation of damage, is not a punishment; it is the repair of a broken balance.

129
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:07.370
And repair, regardless of whose hand caused the break, falls to whoever has the means.

130
00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:13.900
The verse speaks of the ledger of conscience and of the hereafter; the âkile speaks of how an orphaned household will eat.

131
00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:20.110
The first can never be handed over; if the second is not handed over, a victim is left with nothing.

132
00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:25.680
The thinker who best explains the social basis of this logic is probably Ibn Khaldun.

133
00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:37.810
The concept of asabiyyah, which stands at the centre of the Muqaddimah, is the bond of solidarity that keeps a community standing: the thing that defends a person, feeds them, gives them an identity,

134
00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.980
makes them capable of what they could never do alone.

135
00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:45.910
What Ibn Khaldun insists on showing is that this bond is not a one-way gift.

136
00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:50.200
The circle that protects you is also the circle that pays because of you.

137
00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:57.050
The people who will line up behind you when something happens to you are the people who will split the bill when you do something to someone else.

138
00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:02.940
That is the cost of solidarity, and there has never been such a thing as solidarity without cost.

139
00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.750
A similar tension can be seen in a far older text.

140
00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:12.960
In one place the Torah says that God visits the iniquity of the fathers upon the third and fourth generation.

141
00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:20.130
In another place it commands plainly that fathers shall not be put to death for their children, nor children for their fathers.

142
00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:30.500
And in the book of Ezekiel the prophet takes a proverb circulating among the people and breaks it apart: the fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge.

143
00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:36.870
You shall no longer use this saying, he says; the one who dies, dies for his own sin.

144
00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:44.040
Which is to say that thousands of years ago people were already circling the same question, caught between the same two poles.

145
00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:48.490
All three traditions end up in a similar place.

146
00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:55.500
Sin is personal, the reckoning is individual, and no one is born stained because of the family they were born into.

147
00:14:55.500 --> 00:15:06.030
But the consequence, the damage left lying there, does not clear itself away; it goes on standing as something that needs repair, and the obligation of repair falls to the living.

148
00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:09.520
So your grandfather's crime is not your crime.

149
00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:16.850
But what your grandfather left behind is not only a criminal record; it is a house, a name, a story, a position.

150
00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:26.260
And it is a curious fact about human beings that there is a state in which they refuse absolutely to divide this package, and another in which they insist on dividing it.

151
00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.150
Picture a dinner table.

152
00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:34.680
A man who carries his grandfather's photograph in his wallet is telling his children about that man's courage.

153
00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:44.250
He says the name of a great commander in his line with pride, shows his children the inscription of a charitable foundation established by an ancestor he never met on his phone,

154
00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:51.420
and on the anniversary of a battle says "we won" — even if 900 years have passed since that battle.

155
00:15:51.420 --> 00:16:00.990
The link he draws between himself and the man who rode a horse in 1071 is plainly a bond of "we," and nobody asks him to prove it, and nobody finds it strange.

156
00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:07.040
At the same table, ask the same man about a dark page in the past of his family or his country.

157
00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:11.650
The answer changes instantly: I hadn't even been born.

158
00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:14.180
Notice that the sentence is true.

159
00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:19.910
But the same sentence was equally true five minutes earlier, and then it went unused.

160
00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:23.080
This is the sharpest point of the whole question.

161
00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:28.010
If pride flows down the generations without any difficulty, why doesn't shame?

162
00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:39.500
A person cannot read the profitable page of the past as "we" and the costly page as "them." Identity is an account book; to take the assets and refuse the liabilities is not to read the book but to tear it.

163
00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:45.390
The law has no patience at all for this asymmetry, and its answer is exceedingly cold-blooded.

164
00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:49.680
Under the Turkish Civil Code an heir may refuse an inheritance.

165
00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:53.970
Nobody can be forced to accept an inheritance they do not want.

166
00:16:53.970 --> 00:17:00.180
But the rule of refusal is this: an inheritance is a single whole.

167
00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:05.270
You cannot take the house and leave its mortgage, take the shop and leave the shop's debts.

168
00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:10.680
If you accept, you accept all of it; if you refuse, you refuse all of it.

169
00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:17.850
What the law is doing here is not delivering a moral lesson; it is simply making consistency compulsory.

170
00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:23.740
Closing off, with a single article, the trap into which human conscience falls most often.

171
00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:27.550
This is exactly what Jaspers meant by political burden.

172
00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:33.440
And exactly what Arendt meant by the responsibility we carry for things we did not do ourselves.

173
00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:40.770
Both arrive at the same place: belonging to a community has a price, and that price is paid by people who are not guilty.

174
00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:47.940
One example of it is the agreement signed in Luxembourg in 1952, six years after Jaspers's lectures.

175
00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:58.150
Federal Germany undertook to pay 3 billion marks to Israel and 450 million marks to the conference representing Jewish material claims, on a schedule spread over years.

176
00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:06.280
The great majority of the taxpayers who paid that money had been children, or had not yet been born, when the thing they were paying for was done.

177
00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:11.850
Nobody called them guilty; no court was convened, no one was stamped with a mark.

178
00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:20.460
What they were told was something else: you are citizens of this state, this state carried out a destruction, and the money for repair will come from here.

179
00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:23.790
The real lesson of this example is not the money.

180
00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:27.600
The real lesson is how well the distinction works in practice.

181
00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:35.890
Had they chosen the language of collective guilt, they would have ended up at one of two doors: either they would declare an entire people stained from birth,

182
00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:45.620
which is precisely the same form of thought that led to the destruction in the first place, or, as Arendt said, by counting everyone guilty they would leave nobody guilty.

183
00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:54.070
Choosing the language of responsibility instead of guilt was the only way to keep the perpetrator's file open while still placing an obligation on the grandchild.

184
00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:58.840
And this is a distinction that can be applied directly to your own life today.

185
00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:10.650
If there is an injustice in your family's past that someone did to someone, you are not obliged to carry shame; shame is the wrong emotion, because shame is the perpetrator's emotion, and to carry it is to put yourself,

186
00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:13.980
without noticing, in the perpetrator's place.

187
00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:20.510
What you do have to carry is plainer and heavier: is there a consequence of that injustice still standing today?

188
00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:26.880
A confiscated property, a severed tie, an unsaid word, a human being still alive?

189
00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.890
If there is, looking at it is your business.

190
00:19:29.890 --> 00:19:37.540
If there is not, then using the file of the past as a stick against others or a whip against yourself has no moral value whatsoever.

191
00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:41.510
And there is a third thing, perhaps the most lasting of all.

192
00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:47.080
We began by describing the neighbourhood's payment of blood money as though it were an act of oppression.

193
00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:54.250
Yet the hidden assumption of that institution was this: a community genuinely owns what happens on its own street.

194
00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:59.820
A neighbourhood that refuses to pay has, in effect, conceded that the street is not its own.

195
00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:10.350
And a person today who closes off every question about the past with "I hadn't been born" is doing the same thing without realising it: claiming his ancestor's victory on one side,

196
00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:13.840
and abandoning his ancestor's street on the other.

197
00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.690
Your grandfather's crime is not your burden.

198
00:20:16.690 --> 00:20:24.660
But your grandfather's world is your world, and if an account was left open in that world, the hands that can close it are now only your hands.

199
00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:28.310
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

200
00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:31.960
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
